\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.22}{\ignorespaces A sketch of the frequency stabilisation servo. An EOM creates 35\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}MHz sidebands which are used to produce a PDH locking signal. A PZT controls the lenght of the cavity at high frequencies. A heater and a digital loop is used to provide slow control that corrects for frequency drifts associated with change in the NPRO temperature and the room temperature. This is required as otherwise the PZT would run out of range. The open loop transfer function was measured using signals at A and B. The frequency noise was measured using the error point and control signals marked by $c$ and $d$, with this notation corresponding with the notation in Figure \ref  {fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoisemeasurementmathssketch} and Equation (\ref  {eq:ligolaserchaptermeasurement_of_frequency_noise}).\relax }}{56}{figure.caption.65}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoisemeasurementsketch}{{4.22}{56}{A sketch of the frequency stabilisation servo. An EOM creates 35\,MHz sidebands which are used to produce a PDH locking signal. A PZT controls the lenght of the cavity at high frequencies. A heater and a digital loop is used to provide slow control that corrects for frequency drifts associated with change in the NPRO temperature and the room temperature. This is required as otherwise the PZT would run out of range. The open loop transfer function was measured using signals at A and B. The frequency noise was measured using the error point and control signals marked by $c$ and $d$, with this notation corresponding with the notation in Figure \ref {fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoisemeasurementmathssketch} and Equation (\ref {eq:ligolaserchaptermeasurement_of_frequency_noise}).\relax }{figure.caption.65}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.23}{\ignorespaces The servo can be considered as being made of three parts: the optics, the shaping electronics and the PZT. The transfer function of the optics represents how a difference in frequency between the laser and the cavity transforms into a signal after the demodulator. When at resonance and below the cavity pole, this is a flat transfer function that is proportional to the difference in frequency. Since this cavity pole is in the \tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}MHz region and this measurement will only go to the kHz region, the cavity pole can be ignored. The transfer function of the electronics includes everything after the demodulator up to and including the final high power OP amp. The PZT transfer function converts a voltage to a cavity frequency. The PZT acts as a low pass filter due to it having capacitance.\relax }}{57}{figure.caption.67}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoisemeasurementmathssketch}{{4.23}{57}{The servo can be considered as being made of three parts: the optics, the shaping electronics and the PZT. The transfer function of the optics represents how a difference in frequency between the laser and the cavity transforms into a signal after the demodulator. When at resonance and below the cavity pole, this is a flat transfer function that is proportional to the difference in frequency. Since this cavity pole is in the \,MHz region and this measurement will only go to the kHz region, the cavity pole can be ignored. The transfer function of the electronics includes everything after the demodulator up to and including the final high power OP amp. The PZT transfer function converts a voltage to a cavity frequency. The PZT acts as a low pass filter due to it having capacitance.\relax }{figure.caption.67}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{58}{section*.69}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{58}{section*.71}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.24}{\ignorespaces A ramp was applied to the PZT and the photodiode signal was measured before and after demodulation. The demodulated photodiode signal is shown in blue. The photodiode signal is shown in red. Around the peak of the photodiode signal, the slope of the demodulated signal gives the gain of the `optics' transfer function. This can be expressed in units of MHz/V by converting the time axis to a frequency axis. This is done by using the FWHM of the photodiode signal as a reference frequency. The level of the FWHM is indicated by the dashed red line.\relax }}{59}{figure.caption.68}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchaptermixercalibration}{{4.24}{59}{A ramp was applied to the PZT and the photodiode signal was measured before and after demodulation. The demodulated photodiode signal is shown in blue. The photodiode signal is shown in red. Around the peak of the photodiode signal, the slope of the demodulated signal gives the gain of the `optics' transfer function. This can be expressed in units of MHz/V by converting the time axis to a frequency axis. This is done by using the FWHM of the photodiode signal as a reference frequency. The level of the FWHM is indicated by the dashed red line.\relax }{figure.caption.68}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.25}{\ignorespaces A ramp was applied to the PZT and the reflected light from the cavity was measured. The FSR of the cavity is known, so the time in this measurement can be converted to frequency. This gives the PZT transfer function at low frequencies. The ramp signal is shown in blue and the reflected light is shown in red.\relax }}{60}{figure.caption.70}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterpmcscan}{{4.25}{60}{A ramp was applied to the PZT and the reflected light from the cavity was measured. The FSR of the cavity is known, so the time in this measurement can be converted to frequency. This gives the PZT transfer function at low frequencies. The ramp signal is shown in blue and the reflected light is shown in red.\relax }{figure.caption.70}{}}
