\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.27}{\ignorespaces The mixer signal combined with the open loop transfer function is shown in red. This is equivalent to the frequency noise of the system. The PZT signal should agree with this measurement below the unity gain point ($\sim 800$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz). A signal was applied to the PZT at 1111\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz. This is below the corner frequency of the PZT and above the unity gain point for the servo. This should match with the mixer signal. There is a factor of two between these two measurements. This can be attributed to imperfect calibration due to nominal parameters of the PMC being used. The typical noise expected from an NPRO is shown by the dashed black line. Between 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz and 1000\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, there is a bulge in the frequency noise. This is likely due to acoustically driven length noise of the PMC as the PMC is not vacuum sealed.\relax }}{64}{figure.caption.76}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoise}{{4.27}{64}{The mixer signal combined with the open loop transfer function is shown in red. This is equivalent to the frequency noise of the system. The PZT signal should agree with this measurement below the unity gain point ($\sim 800$\,Hz). A signal was applied to the PZT at 1111\,Hz. This is below the corner frequency of the PZT and above the unity gain point for the servo. This should match with the mixer signal. There is a factor of two between these two measurements. This can be attributed to imperfect calibration due to nominal parameters of the PMC being used. The typical noise expected from an NPRO is shown by the dashed black line. Between 100\,Hz and 1000\,Hz, there is a bulge in the frequency noise. This is likely due to acoustically driven length noise of the PMC as the PMC is not vacuum sealed.\relax }{figure.caption.76}{}}
